Yes. The useful move is to treat a kink as a pattern with components, not as one indivisible thing.

You can factor it into things like:

cue
fantasy
emotion
body arousal
power dynamic
novelty/taboo
memory
reward
repetition
context

Then ask what function each piece serves. Sometimes the core is less “the object itself” and more something like novelty, surrender, control, admiration, risk, ritual, embarrassment, safety, intensity, or feeling desired.

From there, there are several possible paths depending on the person’s goal:

understand it
→ map what actually drives it

express it safely
→ keep the desired element, remove harm/non-consent

tame it
→ reduce reinforcement, widen the sexual repertoire, lower compulsivity

redirect it
→ preserve the underlying need but move it into a different cue or context

reduce distress
→ stop treating every appearance of the pattern as a threat or identity verdict

What I would avoid is assuming every kink needs to be “cured.” If it is consensual, lawful, and not causing distress or impairment, it may simply be part of that person’s sexual patterning. If it is unwanted or disruptive, then the goal is usually not brute-force suppression but decomposition, rerouting, and retraining.

So in Pantheon terms:

Kink Hammer

surface kink
→ decompose into latent drivers
→ separate harmless from harmful
→ preserve useful energy
→ reroute unwanted associations
→ test new pattern
→ observe delta

That is much closer to understanding the generator than just fighting the output.